1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,280
Welcome to Rashi in Brief, your weekly podcast

2
00:00:03,320 --> 00:00:05,601
where we explore the Torah portion through the

3
00:00:05,665 --> 00:00:09,281
eyes of the great medieval commentator Rashi, Rabbi

4
00:00:09,361 --> 00:00:11,201
Shlomo Yitzchaki.

5
00:00:11,393 --> 00:00:15,281
This week we're looking at Parshat Noach, which

6
00:00:15,327 --> 00:00:18,241
covers Genesis chapter six verse nine through the

7
00:00:18,301 --> 00:00:21,041
end of chapter eleven, and it's a portion

8
00:00:21,121 --> 00:00:23,841
packed with some of the most dramatic stories

9
00:00:23,894 --> 00:00:25,525
in the entire Torah.

10
00:00:25,665 --> 00:00:28,645
Let's dive in and see what Rashi notices

11
00:00:28,693 --> 00:00:35,630
that we might otherwise miss.Parshat Noach tells the

12
00:00:35,723 --> 00:00:38,270
story of a world gone wrong and God's

13
00:00:38,323 --> 00:00:39,870
decision to start over.

14
00:00:40,137 --> 00:00:43,150
It begins with Noah, described as a righteous

15
00:00:43,230 --> 00:00:45,790
man in his generation, who is commanded to

16
00:00:45,830 --> 00:00:48,510
build an ark to survive a catastrophic flood

17
00:00:48,542 --> 00:00:50,750
that will destroy all life on earth.

18
00:00:50,937 --> 00:00:56,190
After the floodwaters recede, Noah emerges, offers sacrifices,

19
00:00:56,250 --> 00:00:59,710
and God establishes a covenant with humanity, symbolized

20
00:00:59,763 --> 00:01:00,750
by the rainbow.

21
00:01:01,050 --> 00:01:04,030
But the portion doesn't end on that hopeful

22
00:01:04,126 --> 00:01:04,750
note.

23
00:01:04,926 --> 00:01:08,190
Noah plants a vineyard, becomes drunk, and a

24
00:01:08,241 --> 00:01:11,550
disturbing incident occurs involving his son Ham.

25
00:01:11,810 --> 00:01:14,430
The portion then moves on to the Table

26
00:01:14,483 --> 00:01:17,710
of Nations, the descendants of Noah's three sons,

27
00:01:17,770 --> 00:01:19,950
and culminates with the story of the Tower

28
00:01:20,003 --> 00:01:22,590
of Babel, where humanity's attempt to build a

29
00:01:22,643 --> 00:01:25,070
tower to the heavens leads God to scatter

30
00:01:25,118 --> 00:01:28,190
them across the earth and confuse their languages.

31
00:01:28,697 --> 00:01:33,230
Rashi, throughout this portion, is drawn to moments

32
00:01:33,283 --> 00:01:36,830
of moral ambiguity, to words that seem unnecessary

33
00:01:36,910 --> 00:01:39,150
or oddly chosen, and to gaps in the

34
00:01:39,206 --> 00:01:41,554
narrative where the Torah seems to leave something

35
00:01:41,634 --> 00:01:46,456
unsaid, inviting us to look more closely.Let's start

36
00:01:46,488 --> 00:01:48,936
with the very first verse of the portion.

37
00:01:49,096 --> 00:01:52,458
The Torah says, "These are the generations of

38
00:01:52,538 --> 00:01:53,498
Noah.

39
00:01:53,578 --> 00:01:56,298
Noah was a righteous man, perfect in his

40
00:01:56,371 --> 00:02:01,658
generations." Rashi immediately picks up on something curious:

41
00:02:01,698 --> 00:02:05,418
the phrase "in his generations." Why does the

42
00:02:05,419 --> 00:02:08,064
Torah qualify Noah's righteousness this way?

43
00:02:08,204 --> 00:02:10,864
Why not simply say he was righteous?

44
00:02:11,157 --> 00:02:15,024
Rashi presents two interpretations from the Talmud.

45
00:02:15,168 --> 00:02:18,784
Some rabbis read it favorably: if Noah could

46
00:02:18,837 --> 00:02:22,304
be righteous even in a corrupt generation, imagine

47
00:02:22,344 --> 00:02:23,984
how much greater he would have been in

48
00:02:24,024 --> 00:02:25,584
a righteous generation.

49
00:02:25,733 --> 00:02:29,824
Others read it less generously: Noah was only

50
00:02:29,880 --> 00:02:33,344
righteous relative to his wicked contemporaries, but had

51
00:02:33,397 --> 00:02:35,824
he lived in Abraham's time, he wouldn't have

52
00:02:35,856 --> 00:02:37,664
been considered special at all.

53
00:02:37,888 --> 00:02:41,344
What's beautiful here is that Rashi doesn't choose

54
00:02:41,384 --> 00:02:42,864
between the two readings.

55
00:02:42,997 --> 00:02:46,153
He lets the tension stand, teaching us that

56
00:02:46,154 --> 00:02:49,201
the Torah's ambiguity is itself meaningful.

57
00:02:49,388 --> 00:02:51,441
It invites us to think about what it

58
00:02:51,487 --> 00:02:53,761
really means to be a good person —

59
00:02:53,814 --> 00:02:56,641
is goodness absolute, or is it always shaped

60
00:02:56,694 --> 00:03:01,172
by context?Next, let's look at a striking comment

61
00:03:01,252 --> 00:03:04,132
Rashi makes about why God chose a flood

62
00:03:04,194 --> 00:03:06,852
specifically, and why He told Noah to build

63
00:03:06,905 --> 00:03:09,732
an ark rather than simply saving him some

64
00:03:09,772 --> 00:03:10,692
other way.

65
00:03:10,972 --> 00:03:14,065
The Torah describes God's instructions to build the

66
00:03:14,165 --> 00:03:17,345
ark in great detail, and Rashi asks the

67
00:03:17,405 --> 00:03:20,385
obvious question: God could have saved Noah in

68
00:03:20,445 --> 00:03:22,785
any number of ways, so why make him

69
00:03:22,852 --> 00:03:26,465
spend decades constructing this enormous vessel?

70
00:03:26,705 --> 00:03:30,225
Rashi explains that the lengthy building process was

71
00:03:30,265 --> 00:03:32,425
meant to provoke questions.

72
00:03:32,476 --> 00:03:35,105
People would see Noah building and ask what

73
00:03:35,158 --> 00:03:36,105
he was doing.

74
00:03:36,172 --> 00:03:38,065
He would tell them that God was about

75
00:03:38,118 --> 00:03:40,705
to bring a flood because of their wickedness,

76
00:03:40,745 --> 00:03:43,505
and perhaps — just perhaps — they would

77
00:03:43,596 --> 00:03:44,465
repent.

78
00:03:44,585 --> 00:03:47,345
The ark, in other words, was not just

79
00:03:47,425 --> 00:03:48,465
a lifeboat.

80
00:03:48,545 --> 00:03:50,185
It was an invitation.

81
00:03:50,278 --> 00:03:53,665
It was God giving humanity every possible chance

82
00:03:53,718 --> 00:03:55,105
to change course.

83
00:03:55,265 --> 00:03:58,945
This tells us something profound about divine justice

84
00:03:58,998 --> 00:04:03,025
in Rashi's understanding: punishment is never God's first

85
00:04:03,105 --> 00:04:03,586
choice.

86
00:04:03,729 --> 00:04:06,865
Even when destruction is decreed, the door to

87
00:04:06,922 --> 00:04:11,647
repentance remains open until the very last moment.Now

88
00:04:11,687 --> 00:04:13,807
let's turn to a moment after the flood

89
00:04:13,887 --> 00:04:17,487
that reveals something unexpected about Noah himself.

90
00:04:17,727 --> 00:04:21,332
The Torah says, in chapter nine, "And Noah

91
00:04:21,399 --> 00:04:23,092
began to be a man of the soil,

92
00:04:23,192 --> 00:04:26,612
and he planted a vineyard." The Hebrew word

93
00:04:26,672 --> 00:04:30,535
the Torah uses is "vayachel," which can mean

94
00:04:30,695 --> 00:04:33,895
"he began," but Rashi points out that this

95
00:04:33,959 --> 00:04:36,455
word is related to the Hebrew word for

96
00:04:36,535 --> 00:04:41,735
"profane" or "mundane." Rashi says Noah made himself

97
00:04:41,799 --> 00:04:44,215
profane, because the first thing he should have

98
00:04:44,275 --> 00:04:47,735
planted was something more useful or dignified —

99
00:04:47,815 --> 00:04:49,415
not a vineyard.

100
00:04:49,602 --> 00:04:51,895
He should have occupied himself with a different

101
00:04:51,948 --> 00:04:53,095
planting first.

102
00:04:53,303 --> 00:04:56,935
This is a subtle but devastating critique.

103
00:04:57,111 --> 00:05:00,055
Here is the man who survived the destruction

104
00:05:00,108 --> 00:05:03,575
of the entire world, who walked with God,

105
00:05:03,595 --> 00:05:06,455
who was given the chance to rebuild civilization

106
00:05:06,615 --> 00:05:08,855
— and his first act is to plant

107
00:05:08,946 --> 00:05:11,175
grapes so he can make wine.

108
00:05:11,548 --> 00:05:14,455
Rashi is drawing our attention to a kind

109
00:05:14,508 --> 00:05:16,615
of spiritual anticlimax.

110
00:05:16,807 --> 00:05:19,895
Noah was righteous enough to be saved, but

111
00:05:19,915 --> 00:05:21,819
the Torah doesn't let us turn him into

112
00:05:21,859 --> 00:05:23,179
a flawless hero.

113
00:05:23,435 --> 00:05:27,339
Even the best people stumble, and Rashi wants

114
00:05:27,392 --> 00:05:30,995
us to see that honestly.The story gets darker

115
00:05:31,027 --> 00:05:31,635
from there.

116
00:05:31,795 --> 00:05:35,395
Noah becomes drunk and lies uncovered in his

117
00:05:35,475 --> 00:05:36,114
tent.

118
00:05:36,115 --> 00:05:38,675
The Torah tells us that Ham, the father

119
00:05:38,702 --> 00:05:42,275
of Canaan, "saw his father's nakedness" and told

120
00:05:42,315 --> 00:05:43,875
his two brothers outside.

121
00:05:44,195 --> 00:05:48,355
Rashi, drawing on the Talmud in Tractate Sanhedrin,

122
00:05:48,403 --> 00:05:51,875
says that the phrase "saw his father's nakedness"

123
00:05:51,935 --> 00:05:53,715
implies more than mere looking.

124
00:05:53,895 --> 00:05:56,995
The sages suggest that Ham either castrated his

125
00:05:57,086 --> 00:05:59,275
father or violated him.

126
00:05:59,363 --> 00:06:02,355
This is a deeply uncomfortable comment, and Rashi

127
00:06:02,408 --> 00:06:05,075
includes it precisely because the text seems to

128
00:06:05,144 --> 00:06:05,955
demand it.

129
00:06:06,088 --> 00:06:09,157
After all, when Noah wakes up, the Torah

130
00:06:09,205 --> 00:06:11,637
says he "knew what his youngest son had

131
00:06:11,669 --> 00:06:14,357
done to him" — the word "done" implies

132
00:06:14,384 --> 00:06:16,757
an action, not merely an observation.

133
00:06:16,957 --> 00:06:19,877
And Noah's response is to curse not Ham

134
00:06:19,957 --> 00:06:23,637
but Ham's son Canaan, which seems disproportionate if

135
00:06:23,697 --> 00:06:26,357
all Ham did was accidentally see something.

136
00:06:26,650 --> 00:06:30,397
Rashi is teaching us to read carefully, to

137
00:06:30,437 --> 00:06:32,280
notice when the Torah's language hints at something

138
00:06:32,328 --> 00:06:34,360
more severe beneath the surface.

139
00:06:34,520 --> 00:06:37,720
The punishment must fit the crime, and if

140
00:06:37,740 --> 00:06:40,440
the punishment is this harsh, the crime must

141
00:06:40,456 --> 00:06:44,251
have been grievous.Finally, let's look at the Tower

142
00:06:44,304 --> 00:06:46,171
of Babel story near the end of the

143
00:06:46,241 --> 00:06:46,811
portion.

144
00:06:47,931 --> 00:06:50,980
The Torah says the people said, "Come, let

145
00:06:51,033 --> 00:06:52,820
us build a city and a tower with

146
00:06:52,880 --> 00:06:54,980
its top in the heavens, and let us

147
00:06:55,028 --> 00:06:57,300
make for ourselves a name, lest we be

148
00:06:57,356 --> 00:07:00,820
scattered across the face of the earth." Rashi

149
00:07:00,916 --> 00:07:03,300
asks what their sin actually was.

150
00:07:03,420 --> 00:07:07,220
After all, building a city doesn't sound inherently

151
00:07:07,332 --> 00:07:07,860
evil.

152
00:07:08,233 --> 00:07:12,020
Rashi explains that they were rebelling against God

153
00:07:12,180 --> 00:07:14,660
— the phrase "let us make for ourselves

154
00:07:14,740 --> 00:07:18,020
a name" suggests they wanted to wage war

155
00:07:18,080 --> 00:07:21,140
against the divine, to assert their own power

156
00:07:21,200 --> 00:07:23,860
and autonomy in defiance of their Creator.

157
00:07:24,140 --> 00:07:27,780
The irony, which Rashi helps us see, is

158
00:07:27,812 --> 00:07:30,100
that the very thing they feared — being

159
00:07:30,156 --> 00:07:32,660
scattered — is exactly what happens to them

160
00:07:32,740 --> 00:07:34,740
as a consequence of their arrogance.

161
00:07:34,916 --> 00:07:37,940
They tried to prevent their dispersion through a

162
00:07:38,033 --> 00:07:41,620
grand unified project, and their rebellion brought about

163
00:07:41,660 --> 00:07:43,620
the very outcome they dreaded.

164
00:07:43,993 --> 00:07:47,300
It's a powerful lesson about how human attempts

165
00:07:47,353 --> 00:07:50,100
to defy the natural moral order tend to

166
00:07:50,153 --> 00:07:55,139
be self-defeating.Stepping back, we can see several themes

167
00:07:55,169 --> 00:07:57,939
running through Rashi's commentary on this portion.

168
00:07:58,119 --> 00:08:00,899
One is the complexity of righteousness.

169
00:08:01,059 --> 00:08:06,019
Noah is righteous but flawed, praised but qualified,

170
00:08:06,126 --> 00:08:09,219
saved but not spared from his own weaknesses.

171
00:08:09,459 --> 00:08:13,139
Rashi refuses to flatten biblical characters into simple

172
00:08:13,230 --> 00:08:14,819
heroes or villains.

173
00:08:14,949 --> 00:08:18,979
Another theme is divine patience and justice —

174
00:08:19,059 --> 00:08:22,259
God gives humanity chance after chance, whether through

175
00:08:22,279 --> 00:08:24,979
the decades-long construction of the ark or through

176
00:08:24,999 --> 00:08:26,579
the covenant of the rainbow.

177
00:08:26,779 --> 00:08:29,219
And a third theme is the importance of

178
00:08:29,299 --> 00:08:30,579
precise reading.

179
00:08:30,699 --> 00:08:33,379
Again and again, Rashi shows us that a

180
00:08:33,448 --> 00:08:38,179
single unusual word — "in his generations," "vayachel,"

181
00:08:38,299 --> 00:08:41,699
"saw," "done" — can unlock an entire layer

182
00:08:41,752 --> 00:08:44,339
of meaning that a casual reader would miss.

183
00:08:44,479 --> 00:08:47,380
For Rashi, every word in the Torah is

184
00:08:47,431 --> 00:08:50,020
deliberate, and our job as readers is to

185
00:08:50,073 --> 00:08:54,831
honor that deliberateness with our attention.Thank you so

186
00:08:54,879 --> 00:08:57,151
much for joining me for this week's episode

187
00:08:57,231 --> 00:08:58,831
of Rashi in Brief.

188
00:08:59,071 --> 00:09:01,791
Wishing you a wonderful week of learning, and

189
00:09:01,831 --> 00:09:04,111
I'll see you next time when we explore

190
00:09:04,271 --> 00:09:06,431
Parshat Lech Lecha.

